1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,030
Your server writes thousands of lines of logs.

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:07,292
Somewhere in there is the one line that matters. Grep finds it in a blink.

3
00:00:07,762 --> 00:00:11,859
In this video: use grep and regular expressions to analyze text.

4
00:00:15,879 --> 00:00:18,462
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:18,932 --> 00:00:22,048
Use grep and regular expressions to analyze text.

6
00:00:22,518 --> 00:00:24,421
We'll find lines that match a word,

7
00:00:24,441 --> 00:00:27,938
then learn the small pattern symbols that make a search precise.

8
00:00:35,458 --> 00:00:39,705
First, a small practice file, so the examples are easy to follow.

9
00:00:39,725 --> 00:00:42,660
It's a tiny server log called server dot log.

10
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,614
The kit has a one-time block that creates it.

11
00:00:47,084 --> 00:00:51,924
C A T shows the file. Eight lines: some info, a warning, and three errors.

12
00:00:51,944 --> 00:00:54,616
Notice one error is written in lowercase.

13
00:00:58,636 --> 00:01:03,727
Now grep. Grep searches text and prints only the lines that contain your pattern.

14
00:01:03,747 --> 00:01:09,027
Here the pattern is the word ERROR, and the file is server dot log. Two lines match.

15
00:01:11,997 --> 00:01:13,437
Capital letters matter.

16
00:01:13,457 --> 00:01:17,502
Searching for lowercase error finds only the one lowercase line.

17
00:01:20,472 --> 00:01:24,335
Dash i means ignore case. Now all three error lines show up.

18
00:01:28,355 --> 00:01:31,742
Options change what grep does. Dash v means invert:

19
00:01:31,762 --> 00:01:35,951
show every line that does not match. So everything except INFO.

20
00:01:38,921 --> 00:01:43,650
Dash c means count. It prints how many lines match, not the lines themselves.

21
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:45,130
Two lines contain ERROR.

22
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:51,366
Dash n adds line numbers. The warning is on line three.

23
00:01:51,386 --> 00:01:54,352
Handy when you need to open the file at that spot.

24
00:01:58,372 --> 00:02:02,852
Now regular expressions. These are small symbols that describe a pattern.

25
00:02:02,872 --> 00:02:07,475
We put the pattern in single quotes, so the shell leaves the symbols alone.

26
00:02:07,495 --> 00:02:09,324
The caret means start of line.

27
00:02:09,344 --> 00:02:12,837
This finds lines starting with that date and time prefix.

28
00:02:19,307 --> 00:02:23,405
The dollar sign means end of line. Only one line ends with D B.

29
00:02:26,375 --> 00:02:30,998
A dot means any one character, and a star means repeat the thing before it,

30
00:02:31,018 --> 00:02:34,404
any number of times. So dot star means anything at all.

31
00:02:34,424 --> 00:02:37,128
This finds ERROR, then anything, then alice.

32
00:02:40,098 --> 00:02:44,925
Square brackets mean one character from this set. User, space, then a or b.

33
00:02:44,945 --> 00:02:46,670
That matches alice and bob.

34
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:55,348
Dash capital E turns on extended expressions, which add more symbols.

35
00:02:55,368 --> 00:02:59,822
The vertical bar means or. This finds lines with ERROR or WARNING.

36
00:03:02,792 --> 00:03:05,983
The plus sign means one or more of the thing before it.

37
00:03:06,003 --> 00:03:10,537
Here that's one or more digits, then the word percent. It finds the disk line.

38
00:03:14,557 --> 00:03:18,415
Grep can search a whole folder. Dash r means recursive:

39
00:03:18,435 --> 00:03:21,095
look inside every file in that folder.

40
00:03:21,115 --> 00:03:24,973
Here we search slash etc slash ssh for PermitRootLogin.

41
00:03:24,993 --> 00:03:30,473
As a normal user, we get permission denied, because these files are protected.

42
00:03:38,943 --> 00:03:43,058
With sudo, which runs a command as the administrator, grep can read them.

43
00:03:43,078 --> 00:03:47,136
It found the setting, and it's commented out with a hash on this system.

44
00:03:50,106 --> 00:03:51,916
And grep works on a pipe.

45
00:03:51,936 --> 00:03:56,892
L S lists slash etc, and grep keeps only the names containing S S H.

46
00:04:00,912 --> 00:04:04,348
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

47
00:04:04,818 --> 00:04:07,578
Files live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

48
00:04:07,598 --> 00:04:10,110
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

49
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:16,876
Uptime says zero minutes, so this really is a fresh boot.

50
00:04:16,896 --> 00:04:19,960
The checker later confirms the files are still there.

51
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:28,310
Your turn. Make a directory called lab four.

52
00:04:28,330 --> 00:04:32,713
Then use grep with dash i to find the error lines in server dot log.

53
00:04:35,683 --> 00:04:39,260
Send those lines into lab four slash errors dot t x t,

54
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:42,123
using the greater-than sign from last time.

55
00:04:42,143 --> 00:04:46,053
Then run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

56
00:04:53,573 --> 00:04:56,817
Next up: Access remote systems using SSH.

57
00:04:57,287 --> 00:05:00,190
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
